// pond/db — backend-neutral relational store interface (types).
//
// The Go `database/sql` split: this seed declares the `DbDriver`
// interface + the result shapes every driver speaks; concrete
// drivers live in SIBLING seeds (pond/pq postgres-over-TCP,
// pond/sqlite libsqlite3) and structurally satisfy `DbDriver`
// (F.20 — no `impl I for L`). Apps program against the interface
// and inject a driver into the slot, so a store swap is a
// one-line placement change.
//
// **Errors travel as values, not channels.** Hale's F.20
// interface methods can't declare `fallible(E)` (grammar:
// `interface_method_sig` has no fallible clause), and that's
// fine — it lands the Go `(result, error)` shape: every result
// type carries `ok: Bool` + `err: DbError`. A driver's member fn
// does the fallible protocol work internally (calling fallible
// free fns with `or`), catches failure, and packs it into the
// returned struct. Callers branch on `result.ok` / inspect
// `result.err`.
//
// Column data is stringly-typed in v0 (no parametric/row-struct
// generics in Hale): `Row.data` is tab-separated columns,
// `Rows.csv` is newline-separated rows of tab-separated columns.
// Consumers split + parse. Same shape pond/sqlite established.

// DbError — the error value embedded in every result. `kind`
// empty ("") means NO error (success). Vocabulary (backend-
// neutral): "open_failed", "query_failed", "exec_failed",
// "no_row", "constraint", "busy", "io", "protocol",
// "unsupported". `engine_code` carries the backend-native code
// (SQLITE_* result code, or a postgres SQLSTATE mapped to int)
// for callers who branch on engine state. `detail` is the
// human-readable explanation for logs.
type DbError {
    kind: String = "";
    engine_code: Int = 0;
    detail: String = "";
}

// Status — open()/close()-style result: did it work + why not.
type Status {
    ok: Bool = false;
    err: DbError = DbError { };
}

// ExecResult — non-query (INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE/DDL) result.
// `last_insert_rowid` is sqlite-ish; postgres leaves it 0 (use
// RETURNING + query_one for generated keys there).
type ExecResult {
    ok: Bool = false;
    err: DbError = DbError { };
    rows_affected: Int = 0;
    last_insert_rowid: Int = 0;
}

// Row — single-row SELECT result. `data` is tab-separated
// columns. `ok=false` with `err.kind="no_row"` when the query
// returned zero rows (callers don't get a silent empty string).
type Row {
    ok: Bool = false;
    err: DbError = DbError { };
    data: String = "";
}

// Rows — multi-row SELECT result. `csv` is newline-separated
// rows, each tab-separated columns; `n` is the row count.
type Rows {
    ok: Bool = false;
    err: DbError = DbError { };
    csv: String = "";
    n: Int = 0;
}
